A||||18||Gokart||O.K. to run without thermostat?
Z||000000||Gokart||05-31-99||02:06 PM||||Very hot today in New England and my tempature gauge was going past the half way point then when I shut the car off, the needle went haywire and now is stuck all the way to the top! Any suggestions on how I can get the gauge working right again, and is it ok to run without thermostat since now I can't tell if its overheating? TIA
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||05-31-99||02:48 PM||||I would never suggest driving without the thermostat, although a lot of people think (and suggest) otherwise... Just my 2c.
Z||000002||Patrick||05-31-99||03:49 PM||||<br>For what they're worth, you can read my comments on this topic in the thread, "<b>Oil problem?</b>" further down in this forum.<p><p>
Z||000003||theogre||05-31-99||06:29 PM||||If you got that hot, and the fan didn't kick on, make sure the fan even works. read the over heating thread. There is also an expanded article I wrote. go to the general board, and look for the thread "some light reading."<p>running w/o a thermostat at all can cause some problems... mostly due to the engine not reaching it's proper, and even, running temp. These are ususally performance, and emisions problems.<p>
Z||000004||Gokart||05-31-99||08:41 PM||||Thanks for the advice! Its back in! Still don't know what and how to fix my gauge. I'm never going to know if I'm overheating!?
Z||000005||Pretender||05-31-99||11:41 PM||||almost every time i start my 88gt the temp. gauge always sticks up., a good smack or two free's it back down. just a thought..
Z||000006||theogre||06-01-99||07:30 AM||||somewhere here i posted a note about fixing the temp gauge so it doesn't peg when cranking. it also fixes the lamp test for the over temp light.<p>if whacking the dash doesn't jar it loose, you'll have to pull it apart. that's a rather large pain. make sure you do the wiring fix if you have to got this far.... you don't want to have to do it twice.
Z||000007||Lynx||06-01-99||11:30 AM||||Dont Worry. The car has three temp sensors(gauge,fan,ECM). If it does over heat the ECM will pick it up and show you a light. There is a temp guage and a light in the dash. And if you are worrying about the thermostat just buy a new one that you know is good and just get a 160 there only $8 at your local parts store, at least they are here.
Z||000008||Hock||06-01-99||11:53 AM||||if you run without the thermostat you take more of a risk of over heating the engine. this because the radiator has very little time to cool the water before it returns to the engine, resulting in over heating. so my suggestion is to run with the thermostat.
Z||000009||theogre||06-01-99||05:34 PM||||the temp sensor for the idiot light comes on at something even more absurd(125c/257f) than the fan switche(s) (235f for the single speed). The Temp light IS NOT controlled by the ECM. It shares the cavity with the Temp gauge, which is also not ECM controlled.<p>I have never in over 20+ years seen the temp light activate on any car that wasn't boiled over or out of coolant. Do Not trust that lamp to warn you in safe time to shut down.
Z||000010||Eric||06-01-99||06:09 PM||||I was suprised to see that my V6 was well on its way to a meltdown, the other day in traffic.  I saw the gauge jump up to 230f in just a matter of minutes, so I switched on the AC and watched it fall back down.  I guess not all of you have that option, but for those of us who do, it can be a lifesaver in a traffic jam.  I heard of some people installing a manual fan switch inside the car.  Have any of you done this.  If so, I would enjoy hearing how it is installed (what wires to splice, things like that).
Z||000011||theogre||06-01-99||06:24 PM||||there are a few ways to do that. I'm trying to deside which way I want to use.<p>It's mostly personal preferance. At this point, I'm considering locking the fan on permanantly when ever the car runs.<p>basically you find the wire that normally activates the fan on the engine switch, and put in a shunt switch to ground. This method insures that the switch is only activating the fan relay and will only cary a small amount of current. Just where you tap that wire is of little importance.<p>I've been playing with this allot lately. (After making sure everything else in the cooling system is good!) With the fan running, the temp gauge never hits 220, and most of the time it sits on the unlabled mark below 220.<p>I had a long "nap" under the front of the car the other day.... this radiator setup has major problems,,,, like the fact 1/2 the radiator is blocked by the frame. So far I've found about 6 major bugs in the radiator setup.<p>Considering the franenstien collection of parts that is Fiero, I'm suprised there aren't more. 
Z||000012||Cliff Pennock||06-01-99||06:59 PM||||The temp on my 86.5 stays well below 200F, even in a traffic jam! Now on a <b>really</b> hot day, and driving in a traffic jam it sometimes gets to 210, but never more...
Z||000013||Gokart||06-01-99||07:40 PM||||Hey Pretender, thanks it worked! I'm calling Haynes tomorrow, it should be in there!!!
Z||000014||Racer 1||06-02-99||11:24 AM||||I rigged my fan motor to a switch.Its easy also on fiero's if you look at your raidator to the left sholud be a big opening while to the right is flush with the body work.Now if you get some sheet metal and close off the big opening on the left of your raidator then you car will run cooler because air always flows the least resistent path which is to the left of your radiator instead of thru it!You can also get aftermarket racing raidators from several racing distributors!
Z||000015||Cliff Pennock||06-03-99||03:55 PM||||Why is it that always when I tell how good something about my car is, it breaks down a few days later? Just a few messages above I was telling you guys that my car's temp never gets above 200. Well today the car had an overheated engine. Thick white smoke coming from the engine compartment, temp rose from cold to 270 in a few minutes... Fortunately my girlfriend (who was driving it) stopped immediately when she saw the smoke. If she had kept on driving, she would have wrecked the engine.<p>It appears as if the engine is still ok now (it starts/idles fine) and the cause of all this is a probably a broken waterpump. It all started this morning when the engine suddenly heated up to 255 within minutes, but soon after that dropped to normal temperature. She drove it all day without any problems when it suddenly happened again, this time with the thick white smoke (coolant fluid on exhaust?)...
Z||000016||Phil||06-03-99||04:03 PM||||Cliff<br>Better it's coolent ON the exhaust than IN the exhaust
Z||000017||theogre||06-03-99||06:26 PM||||if it's coming out the tail pipe,,,, we're crying for you.... :(<p>If it's comming out the right deck grate,,, it's the water pump or a hose. 
Z||000018||Cliff Pennock||06-04-99||01:30 AM||||That was the first thing I asked my girlfriend, if the smoke was coming frome the deck grate or from the tail pipe. Fortunately it was coming from the deck grate.
